{
  "_meta": {
    "ticket": "OMN-16765",
    "purpose": "Deterministic labelled corpus for evaluating rank fusion against single-leg retrieval. Contains no live-service dependency: both legs' results are pre-computed and checked in, so the only variable under test is the fusion function.",
    "schema": {
      "query_id": "stable identifier, referenced in test failure output",
      "family": "exact_token | paraphrase | agreement",
      "query": "the query string a user would issue",
      "vector": "ranked results from the semantic leg, best first. score is cosine similarity in [0,1].",
      "lexical": "ranked results from the full-text leg, best first. score is Postgres ts_rank, unbounded and NOT comparable to cosine.",
      "labels": "graded relevance. 2 = the correct answer, 1 = related but not the answer, 0 = irrelevant. Documents absent from this map are graded 0.",
      "documents": "per-document timestamps. created_at always present; last_accessed_at null where the model does not carry it or the record has no value."
    },
    "size_note": "24 queries, 8 per family. This deliberately departs from the architecture plan's '50 known error -> resolution pairs' (plan section 7). That 50 was sized for a production precision benchmark over real error/resolution data. This corpus tests a fusion function, where discriminating power comes from covering the cases where the two legs disagree, not from volume. 50 hand-authored synthetic queries would be padding rather than signal. Extending is mechanical if a larger set is ever wanted.",
    "authored_by": "Lakshman Patel",
    "labels_are": "hand-judged, not generated",
    "reference_now": "2026-08-29T12:00:00+00:00",
    "recency_note": "The recency family is built so retrieval alone ranks the WRONG document first -- the stale doc outscores the fresh one on both legs. Only decay corrects it. RC-01..04 discriminate on created_at (both decay variants agree; control arm). RC-05..08 have identical created_at on both documents, so a created_at-only decay cannot separate them at all and only the last_accessed_at term can -- that arm is what measures the cost of the frozen-core limitation. Timestamps are authored, not observed access data. Filler documents are deliberately excluded from this family: a third document at a different age makes the arm measure decay-vs-relevance as well as the variable under test. That interaction is real and is asserted separately in test_decay_can_promote_a_fresh_irrelevant_document."
  },
